(project borge-meta
 (doc "Companion metadata files for .borg specs — machine-generated observations about code reality")
 (status in-progress)
 (no-inline
 )
 (exports write_meta
  read_meta
  update_meta
  meta_of_file
  file_of_meta
 )
 (section purpose
  (doc (|
    .borg files are the source of truth for intent — what the project
    should be. .borg.meta files are the source of truth for reality —
    what the project actually is.

    A .borg.meta file sits next to its .borg sibling. It is sexp-formatted,
    machine-writable, git-tracked, and never hand-edited. It holds
    structured observations that borge commands produce: static analysis
    results, agent findings, module surfaces, timestamps.

    The two-file model keeps the spec clean. Humans and agents author
    .borg files. Machines write .borg.meta files. You can look at either
    file to understand one concern, or look at both to see the gap.
  |))
 )
 (section file-layout
  (doc "Sibling pairing — locality through proximity")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
     Every .borg file may have a .borg.meta sibling:

       borge.borg          ← source of truth (intent)
       borge.borg.meta     ← machine-generated (observations)
       cli.borg
       cli.borg.meta
       sexp.borg
       sexp.borg.meta

     A .borg.meta file is created on first analysis and updated on
     subsequent runs. If no analysis has been run, the file doesn't
     exist — no empty stubs.

     .borg.meta files are git-tracked. This means:
     - You can diff findings between commits
     - You can see when the agent's assessment changed
     - CI can check if findings are stale (timestamp too old)

     .borg.meta files are listed in .gitignore only if the project
     opts out. By default they are tracked.
   |))
  )
 )
 (section format
  (doc "Sexp structure of a .borg.meta file")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
     A .borg.meta file is a valid sexp file with its own top-level
     forms. It is NOT a (project ...) form — it's a flat sequence
     of observation blocks:

     (meta borge
       (analyzed-at 2026-05-15T14:32:00Z)
       (module-surface
         (Spec
           (file lib/borge/spec.ml)
           (exports status_of_string string_of_status project_name
                    find count_sections statuses extract
                    inline_targets has_no_inline status_counts))
         (File_utils
           (file lib/borge/file_utils.ml)
           (exports read_file find_borg_files)))
       (findings
         (finding unspecified-module
           (module Lexer)
           (file lib/sexp/lexer.ml)
           (detail "no .borg section describes this module")
           (confidence high)
           (source static)
           (at 2026-05-15T14:32:00Z))
         (finding partial-implementation
           (section drift)
           (detail "spec says drift checks undocumented bindings but code removed that check")
           (confidence medium)
           (source agent)
           (at 2026-05-15T14:32:00Z)))
       (dune-snapshot
         (library borge_sexp
           (modules ast balance error lexer parse print)
           (public_name borge.sexp))
         (library borge_lib
           (modules spec file_utils check report fmt nodes project lint drift)
           (public_name borge.lib)
           (libraries borge.sexp unix))))

     The (meta ...) form's first argument is the project name from
     the sibling .borg file. This is for human readability — the
     pairing is by filename convention, not by name matching.

     Block types:
     - (analyzed-at ...): ISO 8601 timestamp of last analysis
     - (module-surface ...): per-module export lists
     - (findings ...): structured observations with confidence
     - (dune-snapshot ...): parsed dune file structure

     Additional block types may be added in the future. The parser
     should ignore unknown blocks for forward compatibility.
   |))
  )
 )
 (section module-surface
  (doc "Extracted public interface of each source module")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
     The module surface is the set of names a module exports — its
     public interface, as seen from outside.

     For OCaml with .mli files: the .mli IS the surface. Parse it.

     For OCaml without .mli files (like borge itself): every top-level
     let binding, type definition, and exception is exported. Extract
     these from the .ml source. This is a heuristic — the real surface
     is what the compiler infers — but it's close enough for drift
     detection.

     For other conventions in the future: each convention defines how
     to extract the module surface. Go uses exported names (capitalized).
     C uses header files. The convention declaration in the root .borg
     file determines the extraction method.

     The module surface enables precise drift:
     - "spec says this module exports format_file, but it's not in
        the module surface" → spec drift
     - "module exports check_structural_drift but no spec mentions it"
       → code drift
     - "spec lists 5 functions, module exports 12" → partial coverage
   |))
  )
 )
 (section dune-snapshot
  (doc "Parsed dune file structure for structural drift detection")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
     For the ocaml-dune convention, dune files declare what exists:
     - (library ...) with (modules ...) and (libraries ...)
     - (executable ...) with name and dependencies

     The dune snapshot captures this structure so borge can compare
     the build system's view of the project against the spec's view.

     Drift examples:
     - dune says borge_lib has 9 modules, lib.borg has 7 sections
     - dune says borge_cmd depends on borge_lib, but cli.borg
       doesn't describe that dependency
     - a module appears in dune (modules ...) but has no .borg section

     The snapshot is re-extracted on each analysis run. It's not
     authoritative — the dune file is — but having it in .borg.meta
     means you can diff between commits to see when structure changed.
   |))
  )
 )
 (section findings
  (doc "Structured observations — from static analysis and agent runs")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
     A finding is a single structured observation: something that
     appears to be drift between spec and code.

     Finding types (extensible — new types can be added):

     unspecified-module: module exists in code, not in spec
       fields: module, file

     phantom-spec: spec section describes something not in code
       fields: section

     partial-implementation: spec says implemented, code is incomplete
       fields: section, detail
       source: agent only (can't detect statically without .mli diff)

     status-mismatch: spec status contradicts code reality
       fields: section, spec-status, actual-status
       actual-status is inferred: "stub", "partial", "complete"

     missing-export: spec describes an export that doesn't exist
       fields: section, export, module

     extra-export: module exports something the spec doesn't mention
       fields: module, export

     structural-drift: code organization violates convention
       fields: detail

     Each finding has:
     - confidence: high | medium | low
       high = machine-verified (file doesn't exist, module not in dune)
       medium = heuristic (name matching, surface area comparison)
       low = opinion (agent assessment)
     - source: static | agent
       static = produced by borge drift without LLM
       agent = produced by borge drift --agent
     - at: ISO 8601 timestamp of when the finding was produced
     - detail: free-text explanation (required for agent, optional for static)

     Findings are NOT ground truth. They are observations that may be
     wrong. A human or agent should review before acting on them.
   |))
  )
 )
 (section drift-command-redesign
  (doc "How borge drift changes with .borg.meta")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
     Current drift: heuristic name-matching against filenames.
     Produces ad-hoc output to stdout. No persistence.

     New drift writes .borg.meta files and produces structured output:

     borge drift
       1. Parse dune files → dune-snapshot
       2. Extract module surfaces → module-surface
       3. Cross-reference with .borg sections
       4. Produce static findings
       5. Write .borg.meta (one per .borg file that has findings)
       6. Print human-readable summary to stdout

     borge drift --agent (or borge drift agent)
       1. Run all static drift steps first
       2. For each .borg.meta with findings, assemble context:
          - the .borg section text
          - the corresponding module source
          - the existing findings
       3. Prompt the LLM per section:
          "Here is a spec section and its code. Here are existing
           findings. Review for: partial implementations, silent
           scope changes, missing edge cases, status mismatches.
           Return findings in this format: ..."
       4. Merge agent findings into .borg.meta
          - Keep existing static findings (they're machine-verified)
          - Replace agent findings from previous run (they're opinions)
          - Update analyzed-at timestamp
       5. Print summary with source annotations

     The --agent pass is additive. It doesn't redo static analysis.
     It layers semantic observations on top of structural ones.
   |))
  )
 )
 (section agent-prompt-design
  (doc "Structured output from LLM analysis")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
     The agent prompt must constrain the LLM to return structured
     findings, not prose. This is critical: unstructured LLM output
     can't be diffed, tracked, or acted on programmatically.

     The prompt template (rough sketch):

       You are analyzing a codebase against its specification.
       For each section below, compare the spec description with
       the source code and return findings.

       Spec section: {section_name}
       Status: {spec_status}
       Description: {spec_doc}

       Source file: {module_path}
       Exports: {module_surface}

       Return ONLY a sexp list of findings. Each finding is:
       (finding TYPE
         (section SECTION_NAME)
         (detail EXPLANATION)
         (confidence LEVEL))

       Valid types: partial-implementation, status-mismatch,
                    missing-export, extra-export, phantom-spec
       Valid confidence levels: high, medium, low

       If no findings, return: (findings)

       Example:
       (findings
         (finding partial-implementation
           (section drift)
           (detail "spec mentions undocumented-bindings check but it was removed")
           (confidence high)))

     The agent output is parsed as sexp. Parse failures mean the
     agent produced garbage and the finding is discarded with a
     warning — it does NOT corrupt the .borg.meta file.

     Multiple sections can be batched into one LLM call to reduce
     round-trips, or run one-at-a-time for precision. This should
     be configurable.
   |))
  )
 )
 (section staleness
  (doc "Detecting when metadata is out of date")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
     .borg.meta files become stale when:
     - The .borg source file changed since last analysis
     - A source .ml file changed since last analysis
     - A dune file changed since last analysis

     Staleness detection:
     - Store a content hash of the source files in the .borg.meta
     - On drift run, compare current hashes against stored hashes
     - If any hash differs, that block is stale
     - Print staleness warning even if no other findings

     Alternatively: use git. If the .borg file or its referenced
     source files have commits after analyzed-at, the metadata
     might be stale. This is cheaper than hashing but less precise
     (a commit might not touch the relevant sections).

     For the agent pass specifically: always re-run if metadata is
     stale. Agent findings about old code are worse than no findings.
   |))
  )
 )
 (section writing
  (doc "How .borg.meta files are written and preserved")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
     Machine-written, never hand-edited. But this needs enforcement:

     1. A header comment at the top of every .borg.meta file:
        (; DO NOT EDIT — generated by borge drift. Changes will be overwritten. ;)

     2. borge fmt skips .borg.meta files — they're not specs.
        borge check skips them. borge lint skips them.

     3. The writer preserves formatting: consistent indentation,
        alphabetical ordering where order doesn't matter, one
        finding per line. This makes git diffs useful.

     4. The writer is idempotent: running drift twice with no code
        changes produces the same .borg.meta file. This means
        findings must be sorted and deterministic.

     5. Agent findings merge, not append. If the agent previously
        reported (finding partial-implementation (section drift) ...)
        and now reports the same finding, it's updated in place
        with the new timestamp. If the agent no longer reports it,
        it's removed. The .borg.meta is a snapshot, not a log.
   |))
  )
 )
 (section metrics
  (doc "Static code intelligence stored in .borg.meta companion files")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Every .borg.meta file stores machine-extracted code intelligence
        about its sibling .borg's referenced source files. These metrics
        are generated on analysis, not hand-authored, and enable:

        - borge stats: print a project health dashboard
        - borge review: surface files with declining quality
        - borge generate: inform the LLM about code size and shape

        Metric blocks in .borg.meta:

        (complexity
          (file lib/borge/meta.ml)
          (lines 488)
          (functions 27)
          (average-length 18)
          (max-nesting 4)
          (sentiment-preservation "high"))

        (module-metrics
          (file lib/borge/nodes.ml)
          (lines 112)
          (functions 8)
          (exports 12)
          (mli-coverage 0.75)
          (doc-coverage 0.33))

        (project-summary
          (files 24)
          (total-lines 4100)
          (total-functions 312)
          (plans 13)
          (implemented 19)
          (in-progress 2))

        Extraction methods are convention-specific. For ocaml-dune:
        - lines: physical line count from .ml files
        - functions: top-level let bindings (heuristic for exports)
        - average-length: total lines / function count
        - max-nesting: maximum indentation depth
        - mli-coverage: exports with .mli documentation / total exports
        - doc-coverage: functions with borge-style doc comments / total

        The sentiment-preservation field is LLM-assessed: does the
        spec's description of the code match the code's reality?
        High means the spec is accurate. Low means the code has
        drifted from what the spec claims.

        These metrics enable a new command: borge stats. It reads
        all .borg.meta files, aggregates, and prints:

        Project: borge
        Files: 24  Lines: 4100  Functions: 312
        Coverage: mli 0.75  doc 0.33
        Status: planned 13  implemented 19  in-progress 2
        Quality drift: 1 file with declining coverage (lint.ml)
      |))
  )
  (subsection stats-command
   (doc "Print aggregate code intelligence dashboard")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         borge stats: read all .borg.meta files, aggregate (metrics ...)
         blocks, and print a formatted summary. Show planned vs
         implemented counts, total lines, average function length,
         files with declining quality. This is the first thing a
         developer or agent reads when entering a project.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection review-quality
   (doc "Per-file code quality assessment with LLM")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         borge review FILE: read the spec for the file, read the file
         source, and ask the LLM to assess: Does the implementation
         match the spec description? Are exports documented? Are there
         dead functions? Is error handling present? The review is stored
         as annotated comments in the spec, or in a per-file .review.borg.
         The sentiment-preservation field in .borg.meta is updated.
       |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section future-blocks
  (doc "Extensibility — additional block types")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     The .borg.meta format is designed to grow. Future block types:

     (test-coverage TYPICAL: (module X) (tested-by test_x.ml) (cases 12/15))
       — which modules have test coverage, how complete

     (dependencies (module X) (depends-on Y Z))
       — module dependency graph extracted from source

     (complexity (module X) (lines 230) (functions 15) (max-nesting 4))
       — static complexity metrics

     (agent-session SESSION_ID (started-at ...) (findings ...))
       — if we want to track which agent run produced which findings,
         instead of just flattening into the current snapshot

     Additional blocks must not break the parser. Unknown top-level
     forms inside (meta ...) are preserved on write and ignored on
     read unless the code explicitly handles them.
   |))
  )
 )
 (section semantic-review-integration
  (doc "How semantic review writes to .borg.meta")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     Semantic review (borge review) produces per-function assessments
     that are stored in .borg.meta as a new block type:

     (semantic-review
       (source-file lib/borge/spec.ml)
       (reviewed-at 2026-05-17T10:30:00Z)
       (reviewer agent:semantic-reviewer)
       (functions
         (function name:"status_of_string"
           (doc-present true)
           (doc-accuracy high)
           (signature-match accurate)
           (behavior-coverage complete)
           (structural-issues none)
           (confidence high)
           (checked-at 2026-05-17T10:30:00Z))
         (function name:"project_name"
           (doc-present true)
           (doc-accuracy medium)
           (signature-match accurate)
           (behavior-coverage partial)
           (structural-issues "doc claims X but code does Y when Z")
           (confidence medium)
           (checked-at 2026-05-17T10:30:00Z))))

     Merge strategy: semantic review replaces previous review for the
     same file. Reviews are scoped per-file, not per-project, to allow
     incremental updates. The analyzed-at timestamp in the parent
     (meta ...) block is NOT updated by semantic review — semantic
     review is an additive layer, not a full re-analysis.

     Staleness: a semantic review for file X is stale if:
     - The file's content hash changed since reviewed-at, OR
     - The corresponding .borg section describing this module changed
   |))
  )
  (subsection review-command
   (doc "borge review implementation details")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
       Implementation strategy:

       1. Extraction: Use OCaml's compiler-libs to parse .ml files
          into Parsetree. Extract:
          - Value bindings (let declarations)
          - Type signatures (val_decl in .mli files)
          - Docstrings attached via (** ... *) comments

       2. Batching: Group functions by file. For files with >20
          functions, batch into chunks of 15-20 to respect token
          limits. Sort functions alphabetically for determinism.

       3. Prompt template (per batch):

          You are reviewing OCaml code for alignment between
          documentation and implementation.

          For each function below, assess:
          1. Is there a docstring/doc comment present?
          2. Does the documentation accurately describe the behavior?
          3. Does the implementation match the claimed signature?
          4. Are there edge cases or error paths not documented?

          Return ONLY a sexp list in this exact format:
          (findings
            (function name:"FUNCTION_NAME"
              (doc-present BOOLEAN)
              (doc-accuracy high|medium|low)
              (signature-match accurate|mismatch|unknown)
              (behavior-coverage complete|partial|missing)
              (structural-issues "optional description or 'none'")
              (confidence high|medium|low))
            ...)

          Functions to review:
          {function_list_with_signatures_and_docstrings}

       4. Parse handling: Parse failures mean the LLM produced
          malformed output. Log the failure with the raw response
          for debugging, but do NOT corrupt the .borg.meta. The
          previous review (if any) is preserved.

       5. Merge: Replace any existing (semantic-review ...) block
          for this file with the new findings.

       Commands:
       - borge review FILE: review single file
       - borge review --dir DIR: review all .ml files in directory
       - borge review --all: review all .ml files in project
       - borge review --stale: only review files with stale reviews
     |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section code-documentation-integration
  (doc "How doc coverage tracking integrates with .borg.meta")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     Code documentation tracking counts documented vs undocumented
     bindings in .ml files. Results stored in (documentation ...) block:

     (documentation
       (analyzed-at 2026-05-17T11:00:00Z)
       (file lib/borge/spec.ml)
       (total-bindings 45)
       (documented 38)
       (exempt 2)
       (undocumented 5)
       (coverage-percent 84.4)
       (undocumented-names
         (name internal_helper_1)
         (name internal_helper_2)
         ...))

     Binding detection:
     - Top-level let bindings: let f x = ...
     - let%test bindings: excluded (tests don't need docs)
     - let open M in: excluded (local opens)

     Doc detection (OCaml):
     - (** docstring *) attached to binding
     - (* author note (|description|) *) borg-style comment on prior line
     - (*| description |*) alternative borg format
     - (* exempt doc *) — marks binding as exempt from coverage

     Integration with drift: borge drift --docs flag runs documentation
     analysis and writes .borg.meta files. Drift findings can include
     undocumented-module for files with <50% coverage.

     Integration with stats: borge stats shows coverage aggregate across
     all .borg.meta files.
   |))
  )
  (subsection extraction-strategy
   (doc "How to extract bindings and doc comments from OCaml")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
       Two approaches: compiler-libs (accurate) vs regex (fast).

       Compiler-libs approach (recommended):
       - Use ocaml-common/parsetree to parse .ml files
       - Walk the AST for toplevel Pstr_value bindings
       - Extract docstrings from attributes
       - For .ml files without .mli: all toplevel vals are exports
       - For .ml files with .mli: cross-reference to determine public vs private

       Regex fallback (if compiler-libs is heavy):
       - Match ^let\\s+(rec\\s+)?([a-z][a-zA-Z0-9_]*)
       - Look backward for comment before the let
       - Heuristic: skip lines containing "let%"
       - Less accurate but faster for large codebases

       Current limitation: metrics/stats.ml uses regex and reports 0%
       coverage because it doesn't parse nested comments. This section
       describes the proper implementation using compiler-libs.
     |))
   )
  )
 )
)
